COURSES & TRAINING

Develop Your Skills in Obstacle Sport

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) provides and supports education opportunities for people involved in the development and delivery of obstacle sport.

Our education programme is intended to support coaches, Technical Officials, clubs, volunteers and others who contribute to the sport.

As OCR develops in Ireland, OCRA will continue expanding the range of training and development opportunities available.


Current Education Pathways

OCRA's principal education pathways include:

COACHING

Structured development for people coaching obstacle sport from introductory participation through to national and international performance environments.

Learn more:
https://ocra.ie/education/coaching


TECHNICAL OFFICIALS

Education and practical development for people responsible for applying competition rules and supporting the fair delivery of obstacle sport events.

Learn more:
https://ocra.ie/education/technical-officials


Coaching Courses

OCRA coaching education follows a progressive pathway.

Courses and development opportunities may include:

- OCR Coach — Foundations
- OCR Coach — Participation
- OCR Coach — Performance
- OCR Coach — National
- OCR Coach — International
- OCR Coach — Senior International
- OCR Coach — Master Coach Trainer
- Specialist and continuing development opportunities

Not every level will necessarily be delivered during every training period.

Available courses will be published as dates are confirmed.


Technical Official Training

Technical Official education will provide opportunities for people to learn the rules and practical skills required to officiate obstacle sport competitions.

Training may include:

- Introductory TO education
- Competition rules
- Obstacle judging
- Penalties and classifications
- Practical officiating
- Event operations
- Senior Technical Official development
- Refresher and rules update sessions

Practical event experience may form part of the development pathway.


Supporting Education

Some roles within obstacle sport also require knowledge outside discipline-specific coaching or officiating.

OCRA may therefore provide, facilitate or direct participants towards relevant education in areas including:

- Safeguarding
- First aid
- Disability awareness
- Inclusion
- Anti-doping
- Health and safety
- Athlete welfare
- Club development
- Event operations

Requirements will depend on the role and level of responsibility.


How Courses Are Delivered

Education may be delivered using a combination of:

- In-person workshops
- Online learning
- Practical sessions
- Mentored experience
- Assessment
- Event-based practical development

Using a blended approach allows theoretical education to be combined with the practical experience required within obstacle sport.


